  "sections": [
    {
      "section_id": "orthopaedic_cast_section_1",
      "section_title": "Orthopaedic cast",
      "section_order": 1,
      "subsections": [
        {
          "subsection_id": "orthopaedic_cast_subsection_1",
          "subsection_title": "Introduction",
          "subsection_icon": null,
          "content_groups": [
            {
              "group_id": "orthopaedic_cast_group_1",
              "group_title": "",
              "group_subtitle": null,
              "group_icon": null,
              "blocks": [
                {
                  "type": "image",
                  "assetPath": "assets/resources/content/surgical/orthopaedic_cast_01.webp",
                  "caption": null
                },
                {
                  "type": "image",
                  "assetPath": "assets/resources/content/surgical/orthopaedic_cast_02.webp",
                  "caption": null
                },
                {
                  "type": "image",
                  "assetPath": "assets/resources/content/surgical/orthopaedic_cast_03.webp",
                  "caption": null
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "An orthopaedic cast is a rigid external immobilisation device applied around a body part to maintain the position of bones or joints, protect injured tissues and facilitate healing."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "It is commonly used in the management of fractures, dislocations after reduction, and selected ligament or soft-tissue injuries."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "OSCE identification statement:"
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "“This is an orthopaedic cast, a rigid external immobilisation device used to maintain fracture reduction, restrict movement, protect injured structures and facilitate healing. It may be made from plaster of Paris or synthetic materials such as fibreglass.”"
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "subsection_id": "orthopaedic_cast_subsection_2",
          "subsection_title": "Classification of orthopaedic casts",
          "subsection_icon": null,
          "content_groups": [
            {
              "group_id": "orthopaedic_cast_group_2",
              "group_title": "",
              "group_subtitle": null,
              "group_icon": null,
              "blocks": [
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Casts can be classified by their material, anatomical coverage and clinical purpose."
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "group_id": "orthopaedic_cast_group_3",
              "group_title": "A. According to material",
              "group_subtitle": null,
              "group_icon": null,
              "blocks": [
                {
                  "type": "image",
                  "assetPath": "assets/resources/content/surgical/orthopaedic_cast_04.webp",
                  "caption": null
                },
                {
                  "type": "checklist",
                  "items": [
                    "1. Plaster of Paris (POP) cast"
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Made from gypsum-based plaster bandages that harden after wetting."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Mouldable and generally inexpensive."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Useful when careful contouring is needed."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Heavier and slower to dry than many synthetic casts."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "More vulnerable to damage from moisture."
                },
                {
                  "type": "image",
                  "assetPath": "assets/resources/content/surgical/orthopaedic_cast_05.webp",
                  "caption": null
                },
                {
                  "type": "checklist",
                  "items": [
                    "2. Fibreglass cast"
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Made from resin-impregnated synthetic casting material."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Lightweight and durable."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Usually sets faster than POP."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Often permits better radiographic visualisation."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Can be less easily moulded than POP, and still requires appropriate padding and careful application."
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "group_id": "orthopaedic_cast_group_4",
              "group_title": "B. According to anatomical coverage",
              "group_subtitle": null,
              "group_icon": null,
              "blocks": [
                {
                  "type": "image",
                  "assetPath": "assets/resources/content/surgical/orthopaedic_cast_06.webp",
                  "caption": null
                },
                {
                  "type": "checklist",
                  "items": [
                    "Short-arm cast"
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Extends from below the elbow to the hand, usually leaving the fingers exposed. Used for selected wrist and forearm fractures."
                },
                {
                  "type": "image",
                  "assetPath": "assets/resources/content/surgical/orthopaedic_cast_07.webp",
                  "caption": null
                },
                {
                  "type": "checklist",
                  "items": [
                    "Long-arm cast"
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Extends from the upper arm to the hand. Used for selected forearm fractures and injuries requiring elbow immobilisation."
                },
                {
                  "type": "image",
                  "assetPath": "assets/resources/content/surgical/orthopaedic_cast_08.webp",
                  "caption": null
                },
                {
                  "type": "checklist",
                  "items": [
                    "Short-leg cast"
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Extends from below the knee to the foot. Used for selected ankle, foot and lower-leg injuries."
                },
                {
                  "type": "image",
                  "assetPath": "assets/resources/content/surgical/orthopaedic_cast_09.webp",
                  "caption": null
                },
                {
                  "type": "checklist",
                  "items": [
                    "Long-leg cast"
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Extends from the thigh to the foot and immobilises the knee and ankle. Used for selected injuries involving the knee or lower leg."
                },
                {
                  "type": "image",
                  "assetPath": "assets/resources/content/surgical/orthopaedic_cast_10.webp",
                  "caption": null
                },
                {
                  "type": "checklist",
                  "items": [
                    "Hip spica cast"
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Encompasses the pelvis and one or both lower limbs to varying extents. Used in selected paediatric hip conditions and femoral fractures."
                },
                {
                  "type": "image",
                  "assetPath": "assets/resources/content/surgical/orthopaedic_cast_11.webp",
                  "caption": null
                },
                {
                  "type": "checklist",
                  "items": [
                    "Shoulder spica cast"
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Extends around the chest and shoulder region to immobilise selected shoulder or upper-arm injuries. It is used less commonly in modern practice."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Other specialised casts include thumb-spica casts for selected thumb and wrist injuries and body casts for selected spinal or truncal conditions."
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "group_id": "orthopaedic_cast_group_5",
              "group_title": "C. According to purpose",
              "group_subtitle": null,
              "group_icon": null,
              "blocks": [
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Immobilisation cast: restricts movement to facilitate healing."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Reduction-maintaining cast: maintains alignment after a fracture has been reduced."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Walking cast: designed for weight-bearing when clinically permitted and appropriately reinforced."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Serial cast: replaced at intervals to gradually correct selected contractures or deformities."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Bivalved cast: split longitudinally into two sections to accommodate swelling or allow reassessment."
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "subsection_id": "orthopaedic_cast_subsection_3",
          "subsection_title": "Labelled components of a cast",
          "subsection_icon": null,
          "content_groups": [
            {
              "group_id": "orthopaedic_cast_group_6",
              "group_title": "",
              "group_subtitle": null,
              "group_icon": null,
              "blocks": [
                {
                  "type": "image",
                  "assetPath": "assets/resources/content/surgical/orthopaedic_cast_12.webp",
                  "caption": null
                },
                {
                  "type": "checklist",
                  "items": [
                    "surgeryreference.aofoundation.org"
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "A conventional cast consists of the following layers and features:"
                },
                {
                  "type": "checklist",
                  "items": [
                    "1. Stockinette"
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "A soft tubular fabric placed directly over the skin to provide a smooth interface and protect the skin."
                },
                {
                  "type": "checklist",
                  "items": [
                    "2. Padding"
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Cotton or synthetic padding that protects bony prominences and helps accommodate swelling."
                },
                {
                  "type": "checklist",
                  "items": [
                    "3. Casting material"
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "POP or synthetic casting tape forms the rigid outer shell that provides support."
                },
                {
                  "type": "checklist",
                  "items": [
                    "4. Moulded contours"
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Carefully shaped areas help maintain reduction and support the limb. Excessive pressure over bony prominences must be avoided."
                },
                {
                  "type": "checklist",
                  "items": [
                    "5. Cast edges"
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "The edges are finished or padded to reduce skin irritation. Digits should remain visible whenever possible to permit neurovascular assessment."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Exam tip: The stockinette and padding are essential protective layers; the rigid casting material provides the main structural support."
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "subsection_id": "orthopaedic_cast_subsection_4",
          "subsection_title": "Principles of cast immobilisation",
          "subsection_icon": null,
          "content_groups": [
            {
              "group_id": "orthopaedic_cast_group_7",
              "group_title": "",
              "group_subtitle": null,
              "group_icon": null,
              "blocks": [
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "The cast works by limiting movement at the injured area and maintaining appropriate alignment while healing occurs."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Important principles include:"
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Adequate immobilisation: the cast must control the relevant joints and fracture segments."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Correct alignment: reduction must be achieved when required and maintained by appropriate moulding."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Protection against pressure: adequate padding is necessary, especially over bony prominences."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Allowance for swelling: an acutely swollen limb may require a split cast, backslab or another less restrictive method."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Neurovascular monitoring: circulation, sensation and motor function must be checked before and after application."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Appropriate cast length: the cast should immobilise the structures required for the particular injury without unnecessary restriction."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Not every fracture requires a circumferential cast. A backslab or splint is often preferred initially when swelling is expected."
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "subsection_id": "orthopaedic_cast_subsection_5",
          "subsection_title": "Indications",
          "subsection_icon": null,
          "content_groups": [
            {
              "group_id": "orthopaedic_cast_group_8",
              "group_title": "",
              "group_subtitle": null,
              "group_icon": null,
              "blocks": [
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Orthopaedic casts may be used for:"
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Stable fractures suitable for conservative management."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Fractures after closed reduction, when a cast can maintain alignment."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Selected paediatric fractures."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Selected wrist, forearm, ankle and foot fractures."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Immobilisation after selected dislocations have been reduced."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Protection following selected tendon, ligament or soft-tissue injuries."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Selected postoperative situations."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Serial correction of some deformities or contractures."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Selected paediatric hip and femoral conditions requiring a spica cast."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "The decision depends on fracture stability, displacement, soft-tissue condition, patient age, neurovascular status and the ability to attend follow-up."
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "subsection_id": "orthopaedic_cast_subsection_6",
          "subsection_title": "Advantages",
          "subsection_icon": null,
          "content_groups": [
            {
              "group_id": "orthopaedic_cast_group_9",
              "group_title": "",
              "group_subtitle": null,
              "group_icon": null,
              "blocks": [
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Provides effective immobilisation for suitable injuries."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Avoids the need for internal fixation in selected fractures."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "POP is relatively inexpensive and can be moulded accurately."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Synthetic casts can be lighter and more durable."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Can be applied without an operation in appropriate cases."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Provides protection during healing."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "May allow early mobilisation of unaffected joints."
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "subsection_id": "orthopaedic_cast_subsection_7",
          "subsection_title": "Disadvantages",
          "subsection_icon": null,
          "content_groups": [
            {
              "group_id": "orthopaedic_cast_group_10",
              "group_title": "",
              "group_subtitle": null,
              "group_icon": null,
              "blocks": [
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Can become tight as swelling increases."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "May cause pressure sores, skin irritation or nerve compression."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Restricts movement and may cause stiffness."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Can be heavy, especially when made from POP."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "POP can be damaged by water and requires careful drying."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "May obscure direct inspection of the underlying skin."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Can lose effectiveness if it cracks, softens or becomes loose."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Requires follow-up to ensure that fracture alignment is maintained."
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "subsection_id": "orthopaedic_cast_subsection_8",
          "subsection_title": "Contraindications and important precautions",
          "subsection_icon": null,
          "content_groups": [
            {
              "group_id": "orthopaedic_cast_group_11",
              "group_title": "",
              "group_subtitle": null,
              "group_icon": null,
              "blocks": [
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "A cast is not automatically suitable for every fracture."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Important considerations include:"
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Severe or rapidly increasing swelling: a circumferential cast may increase pressure and compromise circulation."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Suspected compartment syndrome: requires emergency assessment and treatment; casting must not delay care."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Open fractures: require urgent wound assessment and appropriate management. A cast alone is not adequate treatment for a significant open fracture."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Unstable or irreducible fractures: may require operative fixation."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Skin problems: wounds, infection or fragile skin may affect cast choice and padding."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Need for repeated wound inspection: a splint or other removable immobilisation may be more appropriate."
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "group_id": "orthopaedic_cast_group_12",
              "group_title": "High-yield distinction: cast versus backslab",
              "group_subtitle": null,
              "group_icon": null,
              "blocks": [
                {
                  "type": "table",
                  "columns": [
                    "Feature",
                    "Circumferential cast",
                    "Backslab"
                  ],
                  "rows": [
                    [
                      "Construction",
                      "Encloses the limb completely",
                      "Covers only part of the circumference"
                    ],
                    [
                      "Swelling",
                      "Less accommodating",
                      "Better accommodates swelling"
                    ],
                    [
                      "Initial acute injury",
                      "Used selectively",
                      "Often preferred when swelling is expected"
                    ],
                    [
                      "Stability",
                      "Often provides more complete circumferential support",
                      "Depends on design and bandaging"
                    ],
                    [
                      "Inspection",
                      "More difficult without modification",
                      "Generally easier"
                    ]
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "A backslab is not the same as a fully circumferential cast."
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "subsection_id": "orthopaedic_cast_subsection_9",
          "subsection_title": "Basic principles of application",
          "subsection_icon": null,
          "content_groups": [
            {
              "group_id": "orthopaedic_cast_group_13",
              "group_title": "",
              "group_subtitle": null,
              "group_icon": null,
              "blocks": [
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "For OSCE learning; actual casting should be performed by trained personnel with appropriate supervision."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Explain the procedure and obtain consent."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Assess the injury, skin, swelling and distal neurovascular status."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Obtain and review appropriate imaging; reduce the fracture first if indicated."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Position the limb correctly and maintain the required alignment."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Apply stockinette and adequate padding, protecting bony prominences."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Apply the selected casting material evenly, avoiding excessive tightness and pressure points."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Mould the cast appropriately without creating focal pressure."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Finish and pad the edges."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Allow the cast to set according to the manufacturer's instructions; handle a fresh POP cast carefully."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Reassess and document distal neurovascular status."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Confirm alignment radiographically when indicated."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Provide cast-care instructions and arrange follow-up."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Important: Do not use fingertips to apply concentrated pressure to a soft, setting cast. Use the palms to mould it and reduce the risk of pressure points."
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "subsection_id": "orthopaedic_cast_subsection_10",
          "subsection_title": "Complications of an orthopaedic cast",
          "subsection_icon": null,
          "content_groups": [
            {
              "group_id": "orthopaedic_cast_group_14",
              "group_title": "",
              "group_subtitle": null,
              "group_icon": null,
              "blocks": [
                {
                  "type": "table",
                  "columns": [
                    "Complication",
                    "Key features"
                  ],
                  "rows": [
                    [
                      "Compartment syndrome",
                      "Severe escalating pain, pain on passive stretch, tense swelling and possible sensory or motor changes"
                    ],
                    [
                      "Neurovascular compromise",
                      "Numbness, weakness, cold digits, pallor or delayed capillary refill"
                    ],
                    [
                      "Pressure sores",
                      "Localised pain, burning or skin injury over bony prominences"
                    ],
                    [
                      "Nerve compression",
                      "Paresthesia or weakness in the distribution of the affected nerve"
                    ],
                    [
                      "Skin maceration",
                      "Moisture-related skin damage"
                    ],
                    [
                      "Cast tightness",
                      "Increasing pain, swelling or altered distal circulation"
                    ],
                    [
                      "Cast loosening",
                      "Reduced support and possible loss of fracture alignment"
                    ],
                    [
                      "Loss of reduction",
                      "Fracture displacement despite immobilisation"
                    ],
                    [
                      "Joint stiffness",
                      "Reduced range of movement after immobilisation"
                    ],
                    [
                      "Thermal injury",
                      "Heat generated during setting can burn the skin, particularly with excessive layers or poor technique"
                    ],
                    [
                      "Infection",
                      "May occur in associated wounds or damaged skin"
                    ]
                  ]
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "group_id": "orthopaedic_cast_group_15",
              "group_title": "The most important emergency: compartment syndrome",
              "group_subtitle": null,
              "group_icon": null,
              "blocks": [
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Suspect compartment syndrome if a patient in a cast develops:"
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Severe or escalating pain, especially pain disproportionate to the injury."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Pain on passive stretch of the affected muscles."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Tense swelling."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "New paraesthesia or altered sensation."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Pallor, paralysis and absent pulses are late and unreliable signs. Normal pulses do not exclude compartment syndrome."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Urgently escalate suspected compartment syndrome. Constrictive dressings or casts may need immediate release by appropriately trained clinicians, but this must not delay definitive assessment or fasciotomy when indicated."
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "subsection_id": "orthopaedic_cast_subsection_11",
          "subsection_title": "Cast care instructions for patients",
          "subsection_icon": null,
          "content_groups": [
            {
              "group_id": "orthopaedic_cast_group_16",
              "group_title": "",
              "group_subtitle": null,
              "group_icon": null,
              "blocks": [
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Patients should be advised to:"
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Keep the cast dry unless the material and liner are specifically designed for water exposure."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Elevate the limb as advised, especially during the initial swelling period."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Move exposed fingers or toes regularly if permitted."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Keep objects out of the cast; do not scratch the skin with pens or other items."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Avoid cutting, trimming or modifying the cast themselves."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Check exposed digits for colour, warmth, sensation and movement."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Attend follow-up appointments and radiographic reviews."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Seek urgent medical care for increasing pain, numbness, weakness, cold or discoloured digits, excessive swelling, a wet or broken cast, foul smell, fever or discharge."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "A cast should not be assumed safe simply because the fingers or toes remain warm; worsening pain and sensory changes also require attention."
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "subsection_id": "orthopaedic_cast_subsection_12",
          "subsection_title": "Cast removal",
          "subsection_icon": null,
          "content_groups": [
            {
              "group_id": "orthopaedic_cast_group_17",
              "group_title": "",
              "group_subtitle": null,
              "group_icon": null,
              "blocks": [
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Casts are commonly removed with a specialised oscillating cast saw and protective techniques."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Important points:"
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "The saw vibrates rapidly rather than cutting in the same manner as a conventional rotating saw."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Correct technique and appropriate pressure are essential to avoid skin injury."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "A spreader may be used to open the cast after cutting."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Scissors or other tools may be needed to remove padding and stockinette."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "The skin and underlying limb should be inspected after removal."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Stiffness, dry skin and muscle wasting may be present after prolonged immobilisation."
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "subsection_id": "orthopaedic_cast_subsection_13",
          "subsection_title": "Common OSCE viva questions",
          "subsection_icon": null,
          "content_groups": [
            {
              "group_id": "orthopaedic_cast_group_18",
              "group_title": "",
              "group_subtitle": null,
              "group_icon": null,
              "blocks": [
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Q1. What is an orthopaedic cast?"
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "A rigid external device used to immobilise a body part, maintain fracture alignment and facilitate healing."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Q2. Name two common casting materials."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Plaster of Paris and fibreglass."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Q3. What is the difference between a cast and a splint?"
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "A conventional circumferential cast encloses the limb completely, while a splint or backslab provides support without fully encircling it, allowing more accommodation of swelling."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Q4. Why is padding important?"
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "It protects the skin and bony prominences, reduces friction and helps prevent pressure injury."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Q5. What must be checked after cast application?"
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Distal pulses, capillary refill, sensation, motor function, pain and swelling; imaging is used when indicated to verify alignment."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Q6. What is a bivalved cast?"
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "A cast split longitudinally into two sections to allow expansion, accommodate swelling or facilitate reassessment."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Q7. What are the warning signs of compartment syndrome?"
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Severe escalating pain, pain on passive stretch, tense swelling and paraesthesia. Paralysis and absent pulses are late signs."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Q8. Why should a fresh POP cast be handled with the palms?"
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Concentrated fingertip pressure can create indentations that become pressure points and injure the underlying skin."
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
